# LEARNING.LOG

Record of what was learned from processing previous work, preserved so a
future agent can understand and repeat processing that worked — not merely
reproduce a failure and run a fix command. Append-only; preserve history.
User corrections: table below. Self-discovered lessons: runtime section.

A learning record captures, in proportion to the lesson:
1. What was being processed — the task, situation, or problem.
2. How it was processed — the actual sequence of steps and decisions.
3. What worked — the successful approach and why it worked.
4. What was discovered — constraints, pitfalls, corrections, unexpected behavior.
5. What to carry forward — the reusable workflow or rule for similar future work.
6. Evidence — the result or observation that supports the learning.

Failures and successes are both worth preserving. Acceptance test: does the
record preserve enough of the previous experience for a future agent to
understand what was learned and apply the successful processing when a similar
situation occurs? Executable commands are welcome when they ARE the lesson,
but their absence does not invalidate an experience record.

Entry format (2026-11-16 onward): one JSON record per learning —
  { record_type: "learning", learning_id: "LRN-####" (sequential),
    created_at: actual clock ISO-8601, title,
    context: {task, problem},
    steps: [{step, action, reason, observation, result}],
    outcome: {status, description},
    learning: {discovery, reusable_rule},
    evidence: [],
    related_records: {change_refs[], built_refs[], memory_refs[]} }
The six elements map to: context (1), steps (2), outcome (3),
learning.discovery (4), learning.reusable_rule (5), evidence (6).
Pre-format entries below are preserved prose history.

